🧩 Functional Interfaces — FINAL CLARITY
🔑 One sentence that explains EVERYTHING

Functional Interfaces are just pieces of logic passed as data. Streams consume these pieces.

That’s it. Everything else is detail.

🧠 The 4 Functional Interfaces you actually need
Interface	Input	Output	Used where
Predicate<T>	T	boolean	filter, anyMatch, allMatch
Function<T,R>	T	R	map, collect
Consumer<T>	T	void	forEach, peek
Supplier<T>	none	T	lazy creation (rare in streams)

We’ll go one by one.

1️⃣ Predicate — “Should I keep this?”
Definition (simple)

Takes something → returns true or false

Loop version
if (x > 10) {
    // keep
}

Stream version
Predicate<Integer> p = x -> x > 10;

list.stream()
    .filter(p)
    .toList();

Mental rule
if-condition → Predicate → filter

2️⃣ Function — “How should I transform this?”
Definition

Takes something → returns something else

Loop version
int y = x * 2;

Stream version
Function<Integer, Integer> f = x -> x * 2;

list.stream()
    .map(f)
    .toList();

Mental rule
modify value → Function → map

3️⃣ Consumer — “What should I DO with this?”
Definition

Takes something → returns nothing

Loop version
System.out.println(x);

Stream version
Consumer<Integer> c = x -> System.out.println(x);

list.stream()
    .forEach(c);

Mental rule
action only → Consumer → forEach

4️⃣ Supplier — “Give me something”
Definition

Takes nothing → returns something

Example
Supplier<Date> s = () -> new Date();
System.out.println(s.get());

